      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182798",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the set of four finite lists with elements from $A = {0, 1}$, where we know only one element in each list, as shown below (here, $\u2217$ can be any element from $A$).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}List 1: & 1, & \u2217, & \u2217, & \u2217 \\\\ List 2: & \u2217, & 0, & \u2217, & \u2217 \\\\ List 3: & \u2217, & \u2217, & 0, & \u2217 \\\\ List 4: & \u2217, & \u2217, & \u2217, & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConstruct a list that is guaranteed to be different from each of the given lists: $0$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}"
